---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import QrTool from "../tools/QrTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="qr">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        A QR code as real vector geometry at true size, ready to engrave. The one number that decides whether a phone
        can actually read it — <strong class="text-slate-300">how big one module comes out</strong> — is in the status
        bar rather than left for you to discover on the material.
    </Fragment>

    <QrTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </QrTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The module size is the whole game</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A QR code fails in one direction: the squares get too small. Under about
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">0.6 mm</strong> the beam's own width is a large fraction of a
                        module, the edges blur into each other and a phone gives up — and none of that is visible in
                        the drawing. So the module size is a status-bar figure that turns amber, not something buried
                        in a preview. Two ways to fix it: a bigger plate, or less in the code.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Correction is redundancy, not quality</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Error correction does not make the code sharper — it adds modules that let a reader recover
                        from missing ones. On engraved work the case for it is stronger than in print: a scorched
                        corner, a chipped edge and a surface that has been handled for a year are exactly the damage it
                        exists for. <strong class="text-slate-300">M</strong> is the sensible default;
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">Q</strong> for something that gets picked up.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The quiet border is not padding</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        The four modules of blank space around a code are how a scanner works out where the code stops.
                        Readers genuinely fail without them, especially on a busy surface — a grain, a previous
                        engraving, the edge of the piece. The spec asks for four and so does this tool.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">You cannot cut a QR code out of one piece</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    It is the first thing everyone tries. Cut the dark squares out of a plate and the middle of every
                    finder pattern — plus most of the light area — is an enclosed island with nothing holding it, and
                    all of it drops through the bed. There is no bridging scheme that saves it either: the light
                    regions <em>are</em> the code, and a bridge across one changes what it says.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    So cutting is offered as an <strong class="text-slate-300">inlay</strong>: the dark squares come
                    off the bed as loose tiles to drop into a light plate. It works, it looks superb in two woods, and
                    it is a great many small pieces that are not interchangeable — the tool tells you how many before
                    you start. For everything else, engrave: the contrast comes from the burn and nothing can fall out.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Merged into runs</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A version 5 code is over a thousand little squares. Engraved as a thousand separate rectangles
                        the head spends most of the job travelling between them, so runs of dark modules along a row
                        become one rectangle each — the same burnt area in a fraction of the moves. The status bar
                        shows both counts.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The encoding is not ours</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A QR code that is wrong does not look wrong — it looks like a QR code and simply refuses to
                        scan. The only honest way to prove one is right is to decode it, and this project cannot do
                        that in its own tests. So the encoding comes from a twenty-year-old library with no
                        dependencies, and what is tested here is everything done <em>with</em> its answer: that every
                        dark module is covered, that no light one is, and that the code lands where it says it does.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                Test the finished piece with the phone you expect people to use, at the distance you expect them to use
                it from, under the light it will live under. A code that scans on a bench under a lamp and fails on a
                wall in a corridor is the normal outcome, not an unlucky one.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
